{"result": {"id": "chatcmpl-D9XjpVp7giT8Brx7vAZzq9C1vIUaH", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\n    \"title\": \"Regression Analysis Metrics Community\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community encompasses key statistical entities related to regression analysis, including Adjusted R², R², Explained Sum of Squares (ESS), Residual Sum of Squares (RSS), and Total Sum of Squares (TSS). These entities are interrelated, forming a framework for evaluating the performance of regression models and understanding the variance explained by independent variables.\",\n    \"rating\": 8.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is high due to the critical role these metrics play in statistical modeling and decision-making processes across various fields.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Importance of R² in regression analysis\",\n            \"explanation\": \"R², or the coefficient of determination, is a fundamental measure in regression analysis that quantifies the proportion of variance in the dependent variable explained by the independent variables. It serves as a key indicator of model fit, with values ranging from 0 to 1. A higher R² value indicates that a larger proportion of the variance is accounted for by the model, suggesting effective explanatory power. However, it is essential to interpret R² with caution, as a high value does not necessarily imply that the model is appropriate or that the independent variables are causally related to the dependent variable. Additionally, R² can be artificially inflated by adding more predictors, which is why adjusted R² is often preferred in multiple regression contexts. [Data: Entities (495, 1809); Relationships (400, 398)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Adjusted R² in model evaluation\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Adjusted R² is a modified version of R² that adjusts for the number of predictors in a regression model, providing a more accurate measure of model fit. This adjustment is crucial because it prevents the misleading increase in R² that can occur when adding irrelevant predictors. The formula for adjusted R² incorporates the number of predictors and the sample size, ensuring that the metric reflects the true explanatory power of the model. This makes adjusted R² particularly valuable in model selection and comparison, as it helps analysts identify the most parsimonious model that adequately explains the data. [Data: Entities (839, 2738); Relationships (880, 2154)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Explained Sum of Squares (ESS) as a key metric\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Explained Sum of Squares (ESS) quantifies the portion of the total variance in the dependent variable that is explained by the independent variables in a regression model. It is a critical component in assessing the goodness of fit of the model, as a higher ESS indicates that a significant proportion of the variability is accounted for by the model. ESS is calculated as the sum of the squared differences between the predicted values and the mean of the dependent variable. Understanding ESS is essential for evaluating model performance and making informed decisions regarding model selection and improvement. [Data: Entities (493); Relationships (405, 398)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) and its implications\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) measures the variation in the dependent variable that remains unexplained by the regression model. It is calculated as the sum of the squares of the residuals, which are the differences between observed and predicted values. A lower RSS indicates a better fit of the model, as it signifies that the model's predictions are closer to the actual observed values. RSS is crucial for diagnosing model performance and is often used in conjunction with ESS to evaluate the overall fit of the model. Understanding RSS helps analysts identify potential issues with model specification and the need for additional explanatory variables. [Data: Entities (494, 2730); Relationships (1063, 3572)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Total Sum of Squares (TSS) as a benchmark\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Total Sum of Squares (TSS) quantifies the total variation in the dependent variable and serves as a benchmark for assessing the goodness of fit of a regression model. It is calculated as the sum of the squared deviations of the observed values from their mean. TSS is partitioned into two components: ESS and RSS, which together provide a comprehensive view of the model's explanatory power. The relationship among TSS, ESS, and RSS is fundamental in regression analysis, as it allows researchers to evaluate how well their model captures the underlying data structure. Understanding TSS is vital for econometric analysis and for deriving the coefficient of determination, which serves as a key indicator of model fit. [Data: Entities (492); Relationships (398, 400)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null}, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "protected_material_code": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "protected_material_text": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}], "created": 1771167013, "model": "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18", "object": "chat.completion", "service_tier": null, "system_fingerprint": "fp_f97eff32c5", "usage": {"completion_tokens": 968, "prompt_tokens": 7341, "total_tokens": 8309, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 1024}}, "prompt_filter_results": [{"prompt_index": 0, "content_filter_results": {"hate": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "jailbreak": {"filtered": false, "detected": false}, "self_harm": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "sexual": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}, "violence": {"filtered": false, "severity": "safe"}}}]}, "input": {"messages": [{"content": "\nYou are an AI assistant that helps a human analyst to perform general information discovery. Information discovery is the process of identifying and assessing relevant information associated with certain entities (e.g., organizations and individuals) within a network.\n\n# Goal\nWrite a comprehensive report of a community, given a list of entities that belong to the community as well as their relationships and optional associated claims. The report will be used to inform decision-makers about information associated with the community and their potential impact. The content of this report includes an overview of the community's key entities, their legal compliance, technical capabilities, reputation, and noteworthy claims.\n\n# Report Structure\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n839,ADJUSTED R2,\"Adjusted R2 is a modified version of R2 that adjusts for the number of predictors in a regression model, providing a more accurate measure of model fit.\",6\r\n493,ESS,\"**Explained Sum of Squares (ESS)**\n\nExplained Sum of Squares (ESS) is a fundamental concept in regression analysis that quantifies the variation in the dependent variable that is explained by the independent variables included in the model. It is a critical component in assessing the goodness of fit of a regression model and plays a significant role in the overall evaluation of model performance.\n\nMathematically, ESS is defined as the sum of the squared differences between the predicted values (fitted values) and the mean of the dependent variable. The formula for ESS can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nESS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (\\hat{y}_i - \\bar{y})^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) represents the predicted values from the regression model, \\(\\bar{y}\\) is the mean of the observed values of the dependent variable, and \\(n\\) is the number of observations. \n\nThe importance of ESS lies in its ability to provide insight into how well the regression model explains the variability of the dependent variable. A higher ESS indicates that a larger proportion of the total variation is accounted for by the model, suggesting a better fit. In contrast, a lower ESS implies that the model explains less of the variability, which may indicate that the model is inadequate or that important predictors are missing.\n\nESS is part of the total sum of squares (TSS), which is the total variation in the dependent variable, and can be decomposed into two components: ESS and the Residual Sum of Squares (RSS). The relationship can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nTSS = ESS + RSS\n\\]\n\nwhere RSS measures the variation that is not explained by the model, calculated as:\n\n\\[\nRSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(y_i\\) represents the observed values of the dependent variable. The proportion of the total variation explained by the model can be quantified using the coefficient of determination, denoted as \\(R^2\\), which is calculated as:\n\n\\[\nR^2 = \\frac{ESS}{TSS}\n\\]\n\nThis ratio provides a normalized measure of the explanatory power of the model, with values ranging from 0 to 1. An \\(R^2\\) value close to 1 indicates that a significant portion of the variability in the dependent variable is explained by the model, while a value close to 0 suggests that the model does not explain much of the variability.\n\nIn summary, the Explained Sum of Squares (ESS) is a crucial metric in regression analysis that quantifies the variation explained by the independent variables in a model. It is essential for evaluating model performance and understanding the relationship between the predictors and the response variable. By analyzing ESS in conjunction with TSS and RSS, researchers can gain valuable insights into the effectiveness of their regression models and make informed decisions regarding model selection and improvement.\",5\r\n494,RSS,\"Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) is a fundamental concept in regression analysis, serving as a critical measure for assessing the fit of a regression model. Specifically, RSS quantifies the variation in the dependent variable that remains unexplained by the independent variables included in the model. Mathematically, RSS is defined as the sum of the squares of the residuals, which are the differences between the observed values and the values predicted by the regression model.\n\nThe formula for calculating RSS is given by:\n\n\\[\nRSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) represents the observed values of the dependent variable, \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) denotes the predicted values from the regression model, and \\(n\\) is the number of observations. A lower RSS indicates a better fit of the model to the data, as it signifies that the model's predictions are closer to the actual observed values.\n\nThe importance of RSS lies in its role as a diagnostic tool in regression analysis. It helps researchers and analysts evaluate how well their model explains the variability of the dependent variable. By comparing the RSS of different models, one can determine which model provides a better fit to the data. Additionally, RSS is often used in the context of calculating other important statistics, such as the coefficient of determination (R²), which indicates the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that is explained by the independent variables.\n\nIn terms of assumptions, the interpretation of RSS is contingent upon several key assumptions of the regression model, including linearity, independence, homoscedasticity, and normality of residuals. If these assumptions are violated, the RSS may not accurately reflect the model's fit, leading to misleading conclusions. For instance, if the residuals exhibit non-constant variance (heteroscedasticity), the RSS may underestimate the true error, resulting in an overly optimistic assessment of the model's performance.\n\nMoreover, RSS is closely related to other measures of model fit, such as the Total Sum of Squares (TSS) and the Explained Sum of Squares (ESS). The relationship among these measures is expressed as:\n\n\\[\nTSS = ESS + RSS\n\\]\n\nwhere TSS represents the total variation in the dependent variable, and ESS indicates the variation explained by the regression model. This relationship underscores the importance of RSS in the broader context of regression analysis, as it provides insight into the proportion of variance that remains unexplained after accounting for the effects of the independent variables.\n\nIn summary, Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) is a vital statistical measure in regression analysis that quantifies the unexplained variation in the dependent variable. It serves as a key indicator of model fit, with implications for model selection and evaluation. Understanding RSS, along with its assumptions and relationships to other statistical measures, is essential for conducting robust econometric analyses and drawing valid inferences from regression models.\",13\r\n492,TSS,\"Total Sum of Squares (TSS) is a fundamental concept in regression analysis that quantifies the total variation in the dependent variable. It is defined as the sum of the explained sum of squares (ESS) and the residual sum of squares (RSS). Mathematically, TSS can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nTSS = ESS + RSS\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- **ESS (Explained Sum of Squares)** measures the variation explained by the regression model, reflecting how well the independent variables account for the variability in the dependent variable.\n- **RSS (Residual Sum of Squares)** measures the variation that is not explained by the model, indicating the discrepancies between the observed values and the values predicted by the model.\n\nThe importance of TSS lies in its role as a benchmark for assessing the goodness of fit of a regression model. By comparing TSS with ESS and RSS, one can evaluate how much of the total variation in the dependent variable is captured by the model. A higher ESS relative to TSS indicates a better fit, suggesting that the model explains a significant portion of the variance in the dependent variable.\n\nIn regression analysis, TSS is crucial for calculating the coefficient of determination, commonly denoted as \\( R^2 \\), which is defined as:\n\n\\[\nR^2 = \\frac{ESS}{TSS}\n\\]\n\nThis statistic provides insight into the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that is predictable from the independent variables. An \\( R^2 \\) value close to 1 indicates that a large proportion of the variance is explained by the model, while a value close to 0 suggests that the model does not explain much of the variance.\n\nTSS is derived from the observed data and is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nTSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\bar{y})^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( y_i \\) represents the observed values of the dependent variable, \\( \\bar{y} \\) is the mean of these observed values, and \\( n \\) is the number of observations. This formula captures the total squared deviations of the observed values from their mean, thereby quantifying the overall variability in the data.\n\nIn summary, Total Sum of Squares (TSS) is a critical measure in regression analysis that encapsulates the total variance in the dependent variable. It serves as a foundational component for evaluating model performance through the decomposition of variance into explained and unexplained components. Understanding TSS and its relationship with ESS and RSS is essential for interpreting regression results and assessing the effectiveness of predictive models.\",5\r\n2730,ESTIMATED RESIDUALS,\"Estimated Residuals are the differences between observed and predicted values in a regression model, used to assess the model's accuracy.\",1\r\n1682,EQUATION 13.9.2,Equation 13.9.2 describes the formula for calculating the adjusted R2 statistic.,1\r\n495,R SQUARED,\"**R Squared (R²)**\n\nR squared (r²) is a key statistical measure in econometrics that quantifies the proportion of the variance in a dependent variable that can be explained by one or more independent variables in a regression model. It serves as an indicator of the goodness of fit of the model, providing insight into how well the independent variables account for the variability of the dependent variable.\n\nMathematically, R squared is defined as:\n\n\\[\nR^2 = 1 - \\frac{SS_{res}}{SS_{tot}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(SS_{res}\\) is the sum of squares of the residuals (the differences between observed and predicted values), and \\(SS_{tot}\\) is the total sum of squares (the variance of the dependent variable around its mean). The value of R squared ranges from 0 to 1, where:\n\n- An R² of 0 indicates that the independent variables do not explain any of the variability of the dependent variable.\n- An R² of 1 indicates that the independent variables explain all the variability of the dependent variable.\n\nThe importance of R squared lies in its ability to provide a simple and intuitive measure of model performance. A higher R² value suggests a better fit of the model to the data, indicating that the independent variables are effective in explaining the dependent variable's variance. However, it is crucial to note that a high R² does not necessarily imply that the model is appropriate or that the independent variables are causally related to the dependent variable.\n\nR squared is particularly useful in the context of multiple regression analysis, where multiple independent variables are included in the model. In such cases, adjusted R squared is often used to account for the number of predictors in the model, providing a more accurate measure of goodness of fit. Adjusted R squared adjusts the R² value based on the number of independent variables and the sample size, thus preventing the misleading increase in R² that can occur when adding more variables, regardless of their relevance.\n\nThe assumptions underlying the use of R squared include:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables should be linear.\n2. **Homoscedasticity**: The variance of the residuals should be constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n3. **Independence**: The observations should be independent of each other.\n4. **Normality**: The residuals should be normally distributed, particularly for small sample sizes.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to misleading interpretations of R squared. For instance, if the relationship is not linear, R² may underestimate the explanatory power of the model. Similarly, if homoscedasticity is violated, the estimates of R² may be biased, leading to incorrect conclusions about the model's fit.\n\nIn summary, R squared is a fundamental measure in regression analysis that indicates the proportion of variance in the dependent variable explained by the independent variables. While it provides valuable insights into model performance, it should be interpreted with caution, considering the underlying assumptions and the context of the analysis. Adjusted R squared is often preferred in multiple regression settings to provide a more nuanced understanding of model fit.\",1\r\n1809,R²,\"**R² (Coefficient of Determination)**\n\nR², commonly referred to as the coefficient of determination, is a fundamental statistical measure used in regression analysis to quantify the proportion of variance in the dependent variable that can be explained by one or more independent variables. It serves as an essential tool for assessing the goodness of fit of a regression model.\n\nMathematically, R² is defined as:\n\n\\[\nR² = 1 - \\frac{SS_{res}}{SS_{tot}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(SS_{res}\\) is the sum of squares of the residuals (the differences between observed and predicted values), and \\(SS_{tot}\\) is the total sum of squares (the variance of the dependent variable around its mean). This formula illustrates that R² ranges from 0 to 1, where:\n\n- An R² of 0 indicates that the independent variables do not explain any of the variability in the dependent variable.\n- An R² of 1 indicates that the independent variables explain all the variability in the dependent variable.\n\nThe importance of R² lies in its ability to provide a concise summary of the explanatory power of a regression model. A higher R² value suggests a better fit of the model to the data, indicating that a larger proportion of the variance in the dependent variable is accounted for by the independent variables. Conversely, a low R² value may suggest that the model does not adequately capture the underlying relationships in the data.\n\nHowever, it is crucial to interpret R² with caution. While a high R² value can indicate a good fit, it does not necessarily imply that the model is appropriate or that the independent variables are causally related to the dependent variable. Additionally, R² can be artificially inflated by adding more independent variables to the model, regardless of their relevance. This phenomenon is addressed by the adjusted R², which adjusts the R² value based on the number of predictors in the model, providing a more accurate measure of model fit when multiple independent variables are included.\n\nIn summary, R² is a vital statistic in regression analysis that quantifies the proportion of variance in the dependent variable explained by the independent variables. It is essential for evaluating the goodness of fit of regression models, but it should be interpreted alongside other metrics and diagnostic tools to ensure a comprehensive understanding of the model's performance and validity.\",2\r\n2738,R̄²,\"R̄² represents the adjusted coefficient of determination, which adjusts R² for the number of predictors in the model.\",1\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n880,ADJUSTED R2,R2,Adjusted R2 is derived from R2 and provides a more accurate measure of model fit by considering the number of predictors,27\r\n405,ESS,RSS,\"**Entity: Explained Sum of Squares (ESS) and Residual Sum of Squares (RSS)**\n\nThe Explained Sum of Squares (ESS) and Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) are two critical components used to evaluate the fit of a regression model in the context of ordinary least squares (OLS) regression analysis. Together, they contribute to the Total Sum of Squares (TSS), which represents the total variance in the dependent variable.\n\n**Definition and Importance:**\n- **Total Sum of Squares (TSS)** quantifies the total variance in the dependent variable \\( Y \\) and is calculated as:\n  \\[\n  TSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - \\bar{Y})^2\n  \\]\n  where \\( Y_i \\) is the observed value, \\( \\bar{Y} \\) is the mean of the observed values, and \\( n \\) is the number of observations.\n  \n- **Explained Sum of Squares (ESS)** measures the portion of the total variance that is explained by the regression model. It is defined as:\n  \\[\n  ESS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (\\hat{Y}_i - \\bar{Y})^2\n  \\]\n  where \\( \\hat{Y}_i \\) is the predicted value from the regression model. ESS reflects how well the independent variables in the model account for the variability in the dependent variable.\n\n- **Residual Sum of Squares (RSS)**, on the other hand, captures the portion of the total variance that is not explained by the model. It is calculated as:\n  \\[\n  RSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - \\hat{Y}_i)^2\n  \\]\n  RSS represents the error or the unexplained variance in the dependent variable after fitting the model.\n\n**Relationship Between ESS, RSS, and TSS:**\nThe relationship among these three components is fundamental in regression analysis and is expressed by the equation:\n\\[\nTSS = ESS + RSS\n\\]\nThis equation illustrates that the total variance in the dependent variable can be partitioned into the variance explained by the model (ESS) and the variance that remains unexplained (RSS).\n\n**Assumptions and Consequences of Violation:**\nWhile ESS and RSS themselves do not have specific assumptions, their interpretation relies on the underlying assumptions of the OLS regression model, which include:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. **Independence**: The residuals (errors) are independent.\n3. **Homoscedasticity**: The residuals have constant variance across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. **Normality**: The residuals are normally distributed, particularly important for hypothesis testing.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to biased estimates, inefficient estimates, and invalid inference. For instance, if the assumption of homoscedasticity is violated (i.e., heteroscedasticity is present), the RSS may be inflated, leading to an underestimation of the model's fit. Similarly, if the linearity assumption is not met, the ESS may not accurately reflect the true relationship, resulting in misleading conclusions about the explanatory power of the model.\n\n**Interpretation:**\nIn practice, a higher ESS relative to TSS indicates a better fit of the model, suggesting that the independent variables explain a significant portion of the variance in the dependent variable. Conversely, a high RSS indicates that the model fails to capture the underlying patterns in the data, suggesting the need for model refinement or the inclusion of additional explanatory variables.\n\nIn summary, ESS and RSS are essential for assessing the performance of regression models, providing insights into the explained and unexplained variance, and guiding model evaluation and improvement.\",18\r\n1063,RSS,TSS,\"**Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) and Total Sum of Squares (TSS)**\n\nThe Residual Sum of Squares (RSS) and Total Sum of Squares (TSS) are fundamental concepts in regression analysis, particularly in the context of assessing the goodness of fit of a regression model. \n\n**Definitions and Importance:**\n\n1. **Total Sum of Squares (TSS)**: TSS quantifies the total variation in the dependent variable (Y) around its mean. It is calculated as:\n   \\[\n   TSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - \\bar{Y})^2\n   \\]\n   where \\(Y_i\\) represents the observed values, \\(\\bar{Y}\\) is the mean of the observed values, and \\(n\\) is the number of observations. TSS serves as a baseline measure of variability in the data, providing a reference point for evaluating how well a regression model explains this variability.\n\n2. **Residual Sum of Squares (RSS)**: RSS measures the variation in the dependent variable that is not explained by the regression model. It is defined as:\n   \\[\n   RSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - \\hat{Y}_i)^2\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\hat{Y}_i\\) represents the predicted values from the regression model. RSS is crucial for understanding the extent to which the model fails to capture the underlying data patterns.\n\n**Relationship Between RSS and TSS:**\n\nThe relationship between RSS and TSS is central to evaluating the performance of a regression model. The proportion of the total variation explained by the model is captured by the Explained Sum of Squares (ESS), which can be expressed as:\n\\[\nESS = TSS - RSS\n\\]\nThis relationship leads to the coefficient of determination, denoted as \\(R^2\\), which is calculated as:\n\\[\nR^2 = \\frac{ESS}{TSS} = 1 - \\frac{RSS}{TSS}\n\\]\nThe \\(R^2\\) value ranges from 0 to 1, where a value closer to 1 indicates that a larger proportion of the variance in the dependent variable is explained by the independent variables in the model. Conversely, a value closer to 0 suggests that the model does not explain much of the variability.\n\n**Purpose and Applications:**\n\nThe primary purpose of calculating RSS and TSS is to assess the goodness of fit of a regression model. By comparing RSS to TSS, researchers can determine how well the model captures the variability in the data. This assessment is critical in various applications, including economics, finance, and social sciences, where understanding the relationship between variables is essential for decision-making and policy formulation.\n\n**Assumptions and Consequences of Violation:**\n\nWhile RSS and TSS themselves do not have specific assumptions, the regression model that generates these sums does. Key assumptions include:\n\n1. **Linearity**: The relationship between the independent and dependent variables is linear.\n2. **Independence**: The residuals (errors) are independent of each other.\n3. **Homoscedasticity**: The variance of the residuals is constant across all levels of the independent variables.\n4. **Normality**: The residuals are normally distributed.\n\nViolating these assumptions can lead to biased estimates, inefficient predictions, and misleading conclusions about the model's explanatory power. For instance, if the linearity assumption is violated, the RSS may be artificially inflated, leading to a lower \\(R^2\\) and an inaccurate assessment of model fit.\n\nIn summary, RSS and TSS are integral to understanding the effectiveness of regression models in explaining variability in data. Their relationship, encapsulated in the \\(R^2\\) statistic, provides a clear metric for evaluating model performance, while adherence to underlying assumptions ensures the validity of the results derived from these calculations.\",18\r\n3572,ESTIMATED RESIDUALS,RSS,Estimated Residuals are used to calculate the Residual Sum of Squares in regression analysis,14\r\n398,TSS,ESS,\"In the context of regression analysis, two critical components are the Total Sum of Squares (TSS) and the Explained Sum of Squares (ESS). These entities are essential for understanding the variation in the dependent variable and the effectiveness of the regression model.\n\n**Total Sum of Squares (TSS)** is defined as the total variance in the dependent variable, which quantifies the total amount of variation present in the observed data. Mathematically, TSS can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nTSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\bar{y})^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) represents the observed values, \\(\\bar{y}\\) is the mean of the observed values, and \\(n\\) is the number of observations. TSS serves as a benchmark for assessing the performance of a regression model, as it encapsulates all the variability in the dependent variable.\n\nTSS is partitioned into two components: the Explained Sum of Squares (ESS) and the Residual Sum of Squares (RSS). This relationship can be mathematically represented as:\n\n\\[\nTSS = ESS + RSS\n\\]\n\n**Explained Sum of Squares (ESS)**, on the other hand, represents the portion of the total variance that is explained by the regression model. It quantifies how much of the variability in the dependent variable can be attributed to the independent variables included in the model. The formula for ESS is given by:\n\n\\[\nESS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (\\hat{y}_i - \\bar{y})^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) denotes the predicted values from the regression model. ESS is crucial for evaluating the effectiveness of the model, as a higher ESS indicates that the model explains a significant portion of the variance in the dependent variable.\n\nThe relationship between TSS, ESS, and RSS is fundamental in regression analysis. While TSS captures the total variability, ESS indicates the variability explained by the model, and RSS reflects the unexplained variability or the error in the model. The proportion of variance explained by the model can be assessed using the coefficient of determination, \\(R^2\\), which is calculated as:\n\n\\[\nR^2 = \\frac{ESS}{TSS}\n\\]\n\nThis statistic provides insight into the goodness of fit of the regression model, with values closer to 1 indicating a better fit.\n\nUnderstanding TSS and ESS is vital for econometric analysis as they provide a framework for evaluating model performance. The partitioning of TSS into ESS and RSS allows researchers to identify how well their model captures the underlying data structure and to diagnose potential issues related to model specification.\n\nIn summary, TSS and ESS are integral components of regression analysis that facilitate the assessment of model performance. TSS quantifies the total variance in the dependent variable, while ESS measures the variance explained by the regression model. Their relationship, encapsulated in the equation \\(TSS = ESS + RSS\\), is fundamental for understanding the effectiveness of econometric models and for deriving the coefficient of determination, which serves as a key indicator of model fit.\",10\r\n2154,EQUATION 13.9.2,ADJUSTED R2,Equation 13.9.2 describes how to calculate the Adjusted R2 statistic,7\r\n400,TSS,R SQUARED,R squared measures the proportion of variance explained by the regression model relative to the Total Sum of Squares (TSS),6\r\n3598,R²,R̄²,R̄² adjusts R² for the number of predictors in the model,3\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded acc